{
 "id": "2016-nejati-restorative",
 "name": "Restorative Hospital Staff Break Rooms",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the visual assessment in Nejati, Shepley, Rodiek, Lee & Varni (2016), \"Restorative Design Features for Hospital Staff Break Areas: A Multi-Method Study\", Health Environments Research & Design Journal 9(2), 16–35. Photos of two real hospital staff break rooms in Texas were edited in Photoshop CS6 into five versions each: original, with an indoor plant, with a nature artwork, with a window view, and with a balcony. After demographic questions (gender, age, ethnicity, race and education, with the categories of the paper's Table 1), participants see each set of five photos side by side in random order and rate each from 0 to 10 for how effective the room would be in relieving stress and helping them feel more refreshed; photos can be enlarged by clicking. Analysis as in the paper: mean rating per image, one-way ANOVA within each set with Tukey HSD, and the correlation of ratings with the amenity order. Photos are cropped from Figures 2–3 of the paper with credit; the template is removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) only the visual assessment and the demographic section of the original nurse survey are included; the questions on work experience, break patterns, satisfaction with current break areas and future recommendations are not, because the paper does not print them; (2) the original sample was more than 900 members of the Academy of Medical-Surgical Nurses; (3) the scale end labels (0 = not at all effective, 10 = extremely effective) are written for this template; (4) images are small, about 290 px wide, as printed in the paper.",
